  "notes": "Baltimore quartet, and the research calls it \"the deepest half-time ballad lane on this roster\": 56–62 felt, reading 112–124, with a 95–110 uptempo tail that is not modelled here. `halfTime` is true and the snare is `halftime_3` because the entry states the backbeat position outright. The three drum deltas are the entry's own inventory — \"kick on 1\" alone (one hit a bar, syncopation switched off, the genre's and-of-4 habit removed), \"snare on beat 3 only\", and \"no hat pattern beyond a 16th ghost pair before the snare\", which is why the hat lane is near-silent and the snare's ghost probability is the highest in this batch on just two positions (`2a`, `3e`) rather than the genre's four. Percussion is \"fingersnap or rim\", so those are the only two lanes. Harmony is \"gospel quartet voicing with a wide spread… minor 7th / 9th / sus chords, 2-bar vamps\" — an open voicing over a 35-semitone span, the highest sus substitution rate in the batch, and two-chord families held for eight to sixteen beats. The bass is \"sustained synth bass on the root through the bar; sub-heavy, sitting under a very quiet drum bed\", encoded as a single-degree cell with a near-1.0 root follow. ⚠ The research says the countermelody \"is a second lead voice rather than an instrument\"; `fillGapsOnly` is nevertheless left inherited (true) because turning it off makes the counter double the melody's pitch and `coherence.rs` reads that as a second lead — the claim is recorded here instead, expressed as an unusually high counter density. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all velocity and density numbers, `swing.amount` 0.50 and the melody register; the entry marks its key claim `[LOW-CONF]` and the keys here are therefore lane-level rather than named.",
